{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>Creator of heaven and earth.</b> Rashi wrote (Rashi on Genesis 14:19): קוֹנֵה is like עֹשֶׂה (maker); through His making them, He acquired them to be His. These are two aspects, and perhaps it is indeed so — that the term קִנְיָן comes in the sense of making. Thus, 'כִּי אַתָּה קָנִיתָ כִלְיֹתָי' (Psalms 139:13), which parallels 'תְּסֻכֵנִי בְּבֶטֶן אִמִּי'; and likewise, 'הֲלֹא הוּא אָבִיךָ קָּנֶךָ, הוּא עָשְׂךָ וַיְכֹנְנֶךָ' (Deuteronomy 32:6), for the expression קִנְיָן can mean making, and conversely, 'אֲשֶׁר עָשׂוּ בְּחָרָן' (Genesis 12:5) means they acquired, and 'וּמֵאֲשֶׁר לְאָבִינוּ עָשָׂה' (Genesis 31:1) means he acquired. But the correct interpretation is what he further said: He acquired them to be His, for everything that belongs to a person is called his קִנְיָן, and flocks are called מִקְנֶה because they are a person's principal property. And so the language of the Sages (Bava Metzia 10a): 'One who lifts up a found object for another — the other has acquired it'; 'gazing at ownerless property acquires it' (ibid. 118a); 'a person's courtyard acquires for him without his knowledge' (ibid. 11a) — and in every place it comes for them in place of זְכִיָּה, meaning that it is his. This is what Onkelos intended (Onkelos on Genesis 14:19) when he translated 'דְּקִנְיָנֵהּ' and did not say 'קָנָה':"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {0 "The quotation from Psalms 139:13 is given as 'תְּסוּכֵנִי' where the Masoretic text reads 'תְּסֻכֵנִי'; the variant vocalization is retained as it appears in the source."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:21:11.104329257Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}